 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Local Pixel Merging (LPM)
-Local Pixel Merging is a compositing method where the composition operation for each local pixel involves the merging of $k$ sorted ray-segment lists, where $k$ denotes the number of distinct processors that generated ray-segments for that particular pixel [S11, unofficial]. It partitions the screen into $K$ subregions such that each processor is responsible for producing the final image of a distinct subregion through local pixel merging. A binary heap is used for the $k$-way merging of ray-segment lists from the RayBuffer structures [S11, unofficial]. This method is used when projection areas of local subvolumes overlap on the image screen, requiring a global merge operation on ray-segment lists of local RayBuffer structures to composite ray-segments of each pixel in visibility order for the final image [S11, unofficial].
+Local Pixel Merging is a compositing method where the composition operation for each local pixel involves the merging of $k$ sorted ray-segment lists, where $k$ denotes the number of distinct processors that generated ray-segments for that particular pixel [S12, unofficial]. It partitions the screen into $K$ subregions such that each processor is responsible for producing the final image of a distinct subregion through local pixel merging. A binary heap is used for the $k$-way merging of ray-segment lists from the RayBuffer structures [S12, unofficial]. This method is used when projection areas of local subvolumes overlap on the image screen, requiring a global merge operation on ray-segment lists of local RayBuffer structures to composite ray-segments of each pixel in visibility order for the final image [S12, unofficial].
 
 ### Reduced Binary Swap
-Reduced Binary Swap is a variant of Binary Swap that uses only $2^{K-1}$ processors for compositing when $N$ is not a power-of-two ($2^{K-1} < N < 2^K$) [S10]. It requires extra image data exchange in the reduction stage, sending images from $N - 2^{K-1}$ processors to the remaining $2^{K-1}$ processors [S10]. This variant nearly doubles the image blending workload for a large number of processors compared to standard Binary Swap [S10].
+Reduced Binary Swap is a variant of Binary Swap that uses only $2^{K-1}$ processors for compositing when $N$ is not a power-of-two ($2^{K-1} < N < 2^K$) [S11]. It requires extra image data exchange in the reduction stage, sending images from $N - 2^{K-1}$ processors to the remaining $2^{K-1}$ processors [S11]. This variant nearly doubles the image blending workload for a large number of processors compared to standard Binary Swap [S11].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Messages:** Reduces the number of messages exchanged from $N \times (N - 1)$ (direct send) to $N \log N$ [S10].
-*   **Latency:** $l_{sendrecv} \times \log_2 N$ [S10].
-*   **Bandwidth:** $(1/B_{sendrecv}) \times P$ [S10].
-*   **Blending:** $(1/B_{blend}) \times 2 \times P \times (1 - 1/N)$ [S10].
-*   **Average Compositing Time:** Bounded by $t_{BS} \leq p (g + t_c) \cdot 2.43 n^{-2/3} + o \cdot \log n$, where $n$ is the number of processors, $p$ is the number of pixels, $g$ is transmitting time per pixel, $t_c$ is computing time per pixel, and $o$ is communication overhead per transmission [S9].
+*   **Messages:** Reduces the number of messages exchanged from $N \times (N - 1)$ (direct send) to $N \log N$ [S11].
+*   **Latency:** $l_{sendrecv} \times \log_2 N$ [S11].
+*   **Bandwidth:** $(1/B_{sendrecv}) \times P$ [S11].
+*   **Blending:** $(1/B_{blend}) \times 2 \times P \times (1 - 1/N)$ [S11].
+*   **Average Compositing Time:** Bounded by $t_{BS} \leq p (g + t_c) \cdot 2.43 n^{-2/3} + o \cdot \log n$, where $n$ is the number of processors, $p$ is the number of pixels, $g$ is transmitting time per pixel, $t_c$ is computing time per pixel, and $o$ is communication overhead per transmission [S10].
+*   The time complexity is $O(N/2 + N/4 + ... + N/P)$ where $N$ is the number of pixels per processor and $P$ is the number of processors. In the limit as $P \to \infty$, this reduces to $O(N)$ time [S9, unofficial].
 
